            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

We had identified that your house has some excess air leakage and associated heat loss in the following locations:



** The wood chute into the basement



** The 2 x broken (and covered) windows at the back of the house. There are a few other select windows that do not seal well and all 7 of these (includes the 2 x broken ones) are recommended within this report for an upgrade.



** The attic access panel allows leakage around the perimeter and can be sealed with a removeable caulking, such as Draft Attack



** The 6" chimney for the current wood stove (installing a dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe, that is sealed to the back of the wood stove, would help reduce this.



** Exterior wall electrical outlets and switches. These can be draft proofed by installing foam backer pads that you can get from the local hardware store.



Savings: There should be a minimum 10% reduction in overall air leakage achieved from completing the above air sealing that could potentially save $ 195 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 715 in rebates from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. 



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the target outlined above. This grant can increase up to $1,300 based on how much you exceed the target.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are split into 4 categories:

       - Original House (Exposed) = 2x4@16" with R8 or R10 batts (I modeled R8)

       - Original Huse (Adjoined to Garage) = 2x4@16" with R8 or R10 batts (I modeled R8)

       - Front Addition = 2x6@16" with R20 batts

       - Back Addition = 2x6@16" with R20 batts



Don, you had mentioned to me that the house next door has one of your children living in it and that house was similar yo your house. As it is difficult to increase wall insulation levels from the interior or within the wall cavity, that house had exterior rigid insulation added before new siding was installed.



As an FYI, I modeled the addition of 3" (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene foam board insulation to the exterior of all 4 categories of walls for your house ........ including the estimated energy savings &amp; possible grants/rebates to help you decide.



The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program provides 3 tiers of exterior wall insulation rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($1,500 for adding R10 - R14, $2,500 for adding R15 - R19, and $4,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover some of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding. You have recently upgraded all of the windows &amp; doors in your home, so this upgrade is for your future planning.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 982 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.2GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



To qualify for an EEIP exterior wall insulation rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits



For more information regarding the rebate program requirements and application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $4,940 is available for adding R15 insulation to 100% of the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>

We had identified that your house is reasonably air tight and does rely on those 2 x high wall passive air vents in the upper wall of the hallway outside the bedrooms. As the house requires fresh air (primarily when the kitchen range hood fan, a bathroom fan or the clothes dryer is on), outside fresh air is brought in through those 2 vents



Although your house is reasonably air tight, there are a few upgrades that will help to reduce excess air leakage &amp; heat loss:



** The current co-vented chimney &amp; 6" combustion air supply pipe will be eliminated when you install a new high efficiency combi boiler.



** The kitchen range hood fan was not working while I was there, but we did notice (during the blower door test) there was alot of air leakage coming from the wall that it is installed into (so I expect it may not be well installed &amp; sealed)



** The attic access panel allow leakage around the perimeter and can be sealed with a removeable caulking, such as Draft Attack.



** The bathroom fans appear to be worn and allow air leakage. If you choose to replace them with new ENERGY STAR® rated units, these will have better spring loaded dampers inside that help prevent excess heat loss.



Savings: There should be a minimum 10% reduction in overall air leakage achieved from completing the above air sealing (especially if the boiler &amp; hot water are upgraded) that could potentially save $ 294 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 715 in rebates from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. 



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the target outlined above. This grant can increase up to $1,300 based on how much you exceed the target.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

Some of the windows in the house were not sealing well and allowing air leakage. We specifically talked about 7 windows that you are considering an upgrade to:

       - Basement - Front Window

       - Original House - Front Window

       - Original House - Left Bedroom Window (the one I helped you close from the outside)

       - Front Addition - Left Window

       - Back Addition - 3 x windows (2 of them are broken &amp; covered)



Please select ENERGY STAR® full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m²K. For your house,  I modeled a more efficient window with a U-Value no higher than 1.05 W/m²K.



Savings:

By replacing these 7 current windows in your house with ones that meets the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 248 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,275 in rebates (7 windows @ $325 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a 30 kW electric furnace, and supplemented with a 78.4% efficient wood stove that you self-harvest wood for. You mentioned that you have been using the wood stove for a high percentage of the annual heat load ........ burning 7-8 cords of wood per year. You also outlined that, as you get older, it would be nice to have another "green" source of heat for your house. Specifically, we talked about the cold climate Air Source Heat Pumps (ccASHP's) that are eligible for up to a $5,000 rebate from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative ........ and would use "green" hydro-electricity from the Taltson generating station.



I have modeled a central split ccASHP that would provide a minimum HSPF 10 for Climate Region IV, that will be backed up by your current 30 kW electric furnace when temperatures outside fall below the minimum operating temperature of the ccASHP. NRCan regularly updates the eligibility requirements for ccASHP's, so please review the link (a little further down within this section) for these current requirements. The savings outlined below are strictly for the use of the ccASHP as compared to the electric furnace for the full heating season.



You can still choose to reduce your electric heating load (ccASHP or the electric furnace) by using your woodstove during colder times of the winter. Using the circulation fan from the ccASHP/electric furnace to circulate the wood heat throughout your house would help to balance out the temperatures inside various sections of your house.



Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR® qualified ccASHP, that meets the minimum requirements of the Greener Homes program, you could potentially save $ 2,623 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 37.8 GJ.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $2500 is available for smaller Air Source Heat Pumps (ASHP) or cold climate Air Source Heat Pumps (ccASHP). $4000 is available for larger ASHPs and $5000 for large ccASHPs, depending upon the type installed. Speak with an HVAC professional for recommendations on the type of heat pump that is best suited for your home, for example, an air-source heat pump, or if you live in a colder climate, a cold-climate heat pump.



Depending upon the region in which you live and how you heat your home today, the installation of a heat pump in your home may result in higher utility costs. Consult with an HVAC professional in this field to determine if this type of system is practical and the right choice for your circumstances.



To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.



The heating load of your home is approximately 15 kW. Your new heat pump must be sized to supply heat to your entire home. Ask the professional to size your heat pump according to the heating load of your entire home as indicated above.



For more information: Natural Resources Canada has developed a package of materials related to air source heat pump sizing and selection, intended for use by mechanical system designers and renovation contractors. https://www.nrcan.gc.ca/maps-tools-and-publications/tools/modelling-tools/toolkit-for-air-source-heat-pump-sizing-and-selection/23558



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front (SE) facing roof, directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 4,450 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,137 and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and the one in the basement is an older toilet that can be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 1000 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood, 2 x bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.4 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current wood stove is chimney vented, and can be susceptible to back-drafting of combustion. You should consider having a dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe installed that it is directly connected to the back of the stove.



During my assessment of your home, I did not see smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in your home. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

   - Range hood vents outside

   - 2 x bathroom fans that vent outside

   - Clothes Dryer vents outside



If you choose to replace or install any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STAR® qualified units. For a new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18 min, $0.25 per kWh

Oil  - $1.35 per litre 

Propane - $0.90 per litre delivered

Wood - $256.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $6.78 per bag, $289.74 per tonne bulk delivered



